Let the reference decide whether an intersection with it is exclusive

A write through an inline extension method on an opaque alias of Array,

    extension [element](inline buffer: Arr[element]^)
      inline def update(index: Int, value: element): Unit = buffer(index) = value

was rejected wherever the alias is opaque: "Cannot call update method update
of (arr : p.Arr[String]^) & $proxy1.Arr[String] since its capture set {} of
value $proxy1 is read-only". The inliner casts the receiver to the
intersection of its singleton type and its type as seen through the proxy
of the alias's prefix. The second operand shows the alias to be an array,
so that the value is stateful, but has no capture set, and the capture set
of an intersection is the meet of its operands': empty, however exclusively
`arr` is held.

A value of type `x.type & T` is `x`. Mutability.exclusivityInContext now
takes whether the value is stateful from the intersection, and whether it
may be written through from `x`. A reference which is read-only is still
refused, and is now refused here too where `T` alone says it is stateful.

Found in Soundness's proscenium.Array (Proscala issue 64).

